  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show a \nsprawling surface high pressure system centered from northern \nIllinois to Minneapolis early this morning. Clear skies, light to \ncalm winds, and quiet conditions prevail across north- central and\nnortheast Wisconsin, with early morning surface temperatures \nranging from the middle 40s across the northwoods to the upper 50s\nnear the Fox Valley and Lakeshore. This surface high is \nmaintaining tranquil weather across much of the Upper Midwest, \nextending northwestward into the western Plains and central \nCanada. \n\nAs the high pressure center slides southeast today, thermal \ntroughing will shift east of the region, allowing low- level \ntemperatures to moderate back to near-normal mid-summer values. \nDiurnal heating will prompt fair-weather cumulus to build around \n15Z to 17Z with initial bases around 4000 to 5000 feet, rising to \n6000 to 8000 feet in the afternoon before clearing early this \nevening. Afternoon high temperatures today will reach the upper \n70s to lower 80s. Light westerly winds this morning will turn \nsouthwest to south at 5 to 10 knots during the afternoon, with a \nfew localized gusts up to 15 knots. Looking upstream over the next\n36 to 48 hours, an upper-level ridge building northeastward from \nthe Plains into the northern Mississippi Valley will maintain warm\nadvection while keeping the primary convective storm track well \ndisplaced to the northwest over the northern Plains through \nFriday.\n\nThunderstorm potential increases over the weekend as an active \nnorthwest flow regime establishes itself on the eastern periphery \nof the building upper ridge. On Saturday, a weak mid-level \nshortwave impulse and an accompanying decaying cold front will \ntrack across the region. Instability will build to 1000 to 2000 \nJ/kg over north-central Wisconsin by Saturday afternoon, which \nwhen combined with 30 to 35 knots of deep-layer shear will create \nan environment favorable for strong storm development. \nPrecipitation focus appears highest over far northern Wisconsin \nnear stronger low-level convergence, though activity is expected \nto show a diminishing trend as it pushes east. Sunday presents the\nhighest potential for organized severe weather across the region \nas a warm front lifts toward central Wisconsin beneath strong warm\nadvection aloft. An airmass characterized by 1500 to 2500 J/kg of\nCAPE and 40 knots of deep-layer shear beneath a 90th percentile \n500mb jet streak will provide a favorable parameter space for \nsevere storms. Mid-level capping may serve as a hindrance to \nconvective initiation, but any storms capable of breaching the cap\nwill have potential to become severe. Storm chances linger into \nMonday as a stronger upper shortwave trough and surface cold front\ntraverse the Great Lakes.\n\nAlong with returning storm chances, a hot and humid airmass will \nbuild into the state for the late weekend. High temperatures in \nthe low to mid 80s on Friday and Saturday will climb into the \nupper 80s to lower 90s on Sunday and Monday. Surface dewpoints \nrising into the upper 60s and lower 70s will produce afternoon \nheat indices in the middle 90s across central and east-central \nWisconsin. While convective debris and thunderstorm coverage may \noffer localized temperature relief at times, the heat and humidity\nwill create uncomfortable conditions. A cold front passing Monday\nnight will usher in cooler and drier air for Tuesday and \nWednesday, bringing high temperatures back to near normal in the \nupper 70s and lower 80s.",
